  "answer_text": "Despite his name, Flounder from Disney\u2019s *The Little Mermaid* is **not actually a flounder**. Real flounders are flat, camouflaged, bottom-dwelling fish with both of their eyes on one side of their head\u2014which looks nothing like Ariel's bright and bubbly best friend!\n\nInstead, Flounder's species depends on which version of the movie you are watching:\n\n### 1. The 2023 Live-Action Remake: A **Sergeant Major Fish**\nIn the 2023 live-action film, Flounder\u2019s design was officially modeled after a **Sergeant Major** (*Abudefduf saxatilis*). \n* **About the fish:** The Sergeant Major is a species of tropical damselfish. They are known for their oval-shaped, slightly compressed bodies, yellow-hued backs, and five dark vertical stripes\u2014which perfectly matches Flounder\u2019s signature stripes. \n\n### 2. The 1989 Animated Film: A **Fictional Tropical Reef Fish** (often linked to an **Angelfish**)\nIn the original animated classic, Flounder was not drawn to represent one specific, real-world species. Animators designed him as a generic, round, brightly colored tropical fish. \n* **The Angelfish connection:** Because of his vibrant yellow and blue coloring and rounded body shape, marine biologists and fans have historically compared his original look to a **blue angelfish** or other colorful reef-dwelling fish.\n* **The \"Guppy\" nickname:** Throughout the franchise, other characters (and Ariel herself) frequently tease Flounder by calling him a \"guppy\" because of his cowardly nature. While some viewers took this literally, he is physically not a guppy (which are tiny, freshwater fish).",
